  "reasoning": [
    {
      "text": "The planner still walks components in order, accumulating persistent storage and taking the maximum temporary overhead per device. For each component it subtracts both from the current budget and clips at zero. If all devices have zero room, it now grows the working budgets by floor(11m/10) and retries before applying device limits. This is a way to keep estimating past budget exhaustion, not a guarantee that the plan fits the original budgets. It neither moves earlier assignments nor validates the new estimate against the budgets.",
      "basis": "source",
      "evidence": ["call_0748aefa4699457893b8fe03d96dee12", "call_2b22605aab3745d3bd07300945d5faf3"]
    },
    {
      "text": "The one-device rule changes placement, not just the number of participants: it keeps slot zero, whereas the old rule kept the slot with the most free room. Other non-None limits still use the bounded top-k helper. Pinning makes one-device placement predictable at the cost of ignoring spare room on other devices; the diff does not establish why slot zero was chosen.",
      "basis": "inference",
      "evidence": ["call_0748aefa4699457893b8fe03d96dee12", "call_2b22605aab3745d3bd07300945d5faf3"]
    },
    {
      "text": "Attention's grouping and the compiler's range scaling are coupled. Grouping reduces the number of units passed to the allocator; multiplying the cumulative boundaries by the number of heads per group puts the plan back in KV-head coordinates. The 128-channel constraint is consistent with the parent Linear default padding and EXL3 quantizer's output-width divisibility check. That is evidence for an alignment rationale, not proof that all attention backends need the restriction or that the resulting shards can already be loaded.",
